Please provide the following information. The more we know about your system and use case, the more easily and likely we can help.

### Description of the problem / feature request / question:

Modifying headers do not trigger a rebuild of dependent source files (at least not by default / on Windows). This has caused me size mismatches between structs and resulting heap and stack corruption and crashes. If I change a header to just contain #error, the program doesn't recompile anything. As a routine I always do a full rebuild whenever my app crashes, and most of the time (so far) that has fixed the root cause.

I have a simple repro below, but in essence updates to header files will only be observed by files that are otherwise rebuilt. If there's some kind of header-check setting that need to go in here, please let me know, but if so, this seems like a crazy dangerous default to me. Not even the cc_library target that declares the header will be rebuilt if only the header is changed.

### If possible, provide a minimal example to reproduce the problem:

A minimal repro would be main.cc including main.h. If you change the contents of main.h to be #error and build again, the .exe is already built and files depending on the header won't be recompiled.

A slightly more complicated (but interesting) example contain a main target and a library. They both depend on the same header and are already built. After they are built we change the header (modifying the size of a struct), and then change something inside the library source files. This library will then be compiled with the updated struct size, while the main target will use the old struct size and layout.

